  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">

      <p>Avigdor Edelstein submitted his formal Likud party membership resignation on 4 July, effective immediately, citing what his written statement described as "conditions that no longer permit Zionist governance." Speaker Ohana's office confirmed receipt of the resignation and circulated a protocol memo to coalition whips; the Knesset secretary-general logged the change to the 25th Knesset electoral rolls the same day. The coalition tally stood at 61 seats on 3 July at close of plenary business; the Edelstein departure brought it to 60 — one below the minimum governing-majority threshold. No 2nd or 3rd reading of the dissolution bill appeared on the plenum weekly schedule published 6 July, leaving F6's YES resolution path with no viable scheduling mechanism in the remaining nine-day window. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">F7</span></p>

      <p>Pakistani Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif arrived in Tehran on 3 July with Foreign Minister Ishaq Dar and Army Chief General Asim Munir for a combined bilateral state visit and Khamenei funeral delegation. Pakistan's Foreign Ministry issued a press briefing on 2 July, published on the ministry's official portal and carried by AP, explicitly stating that "the next meeting [on MOU implementation] will be scheduled at the earliest possible time following the funeral processions." Qatari MFA issued a three-line readout on 2 July characterising Doha technical-level contacts of 30 June–2 July as producing "positive progress on MOU implementation modalities." Both readouts constitute named-mediator confirmation that the facilitation framework remained operative as of 5 July. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span></p>

      <p>France24 Persian service reported on 5 July that Iran's Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei did not appear at the funeral ceremonies for a second consecutive day; Agence France-Presse confirmed the absence via two Tehran-based staff correspondents. IRIB broadcast all 5 July funeral proceedings — totalling approximately seven hours across its main and news channels — without any live or recorded video footage of Mojtaba Khamenei; anchor-read texts and still photographs from March were the only representations of the Supreme Leader during the broadcast. The July 5 funeral proceedings constituted the highest-profile occasion available for a first public appearance since his election, given the presence of heads of state from China, Russia, Turkey, and at least eight Gulf delegations. → moved <span class="cite">F12</span>, <span class="cite">F8</span></p>

      <p>Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps VHF Channel 16 route-restriction advisories, issued 25 June, remained confirmed operative as of 5 July according to two OSINT AIS tracking services. Iran's government announced a navigation fee for commercial transits through the Strait of Hormuz; the US Treasury Department sanctioned the Persian Gulf Strait Authority under Executive Order 13224 on 1 July. Tehran did not issue any formal suspension of the fee in the 1–6 July period. IMF PortWatch Hormuz 7-day moving average stood at approximately 28–34 vessels/day as of 5 July, above the 30/day floor but well below the 50/day threshold. A single-day count of 39 vessels on 3 July declined back to approximately 35 by 5 July following renewed IRGC advisories, confirming no structural transit gain. → moved <span class="cite">F9</span></p>

      <p>Day 3 of the estimated 6-day Khamenei state funeral cycle concluded on 6 July without any reported diplomatic incident or IDF kinetic action against Iranian territory. The Islamabad MOU foreign-ministry monitoring mechanism issued a routine "no change" advisory on 5 July, confirmed in a joint readout by Qatari and Pakistani MFAs. More than 100 national delegations attended the funeral, including heads of state from China, Russia, Turkey, Iraq, and the UAE. No IDF spokesperson or CENTCOM statement confirming strikes on Iranian territory appeared in the 4–6 July window. → supports <span class="cite">F10</span></p>

    </div>
  </div>

