  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>

      <p>IRNA, IRIB, and Tasnim have dedicated the bulk of editorial space since 4 July to the funeral, with anchor-read eulogies and historical retrospectives running across all prime-time slots. Khamenei's governance legacy appears across three repeated narrative threads: nuclear steadfastness, resistance-axis continuity, and what Kayhan labels "strategic patience before the Great Victory." State-media coverage gives deliberate editorial prominence to the international funeral delegations — Russia, China, Turkey, and Gulf states — as external validation of the resistance framework, a framing absent from Farsi-language opposition outlets and independent Persian-language broadcasters. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>IRNA ran four separate items on the Islamabad MOU between 4 and 6 July, all characterising it as "Iran's diplomatic achievement under the Imam's final guidance," embedding the MOU within the funeral narrative rather than as a standalone outcome. Press TV's English-language desk ran parallel coverage that dropped the "under the Imam's guidance" framing, instead describing the MOU as a "pragmatic arrangement reached under maximum pressure" — a noticeably different valence for international audiences. Both framings consistently describe the talks as ongoing and the framework as durable. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span></p>

      <p>The succession track is absent from state media in any direct form: all coverage refers to "the leadership" or "the Supreme Leader's office" without naming an individual, and no transition timeline has been articulated. Kayhan and IRIB afternoon editorial segments on 5 July cited Khomeini-era transition procedures as the operative precedent — a reference that in 1989 produced a 72-hour interregnum before the Assembly of Experts convened. The blanket editorial silence on Mojtaba Khamenei by name, across five consecutive days of wall-to-wall state-media funeral coverage, is the strongest available evidence that the governance uncertainty built into the forecast remains unresolved. → supports <span class="cite">F12</span></p>

    </div>
  </div>

